WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.835
Safe file handling in Python requires treating paths, filenames, file
contents, metadata, permissions, and storage locations as security-
relevant.

2
00:00:08.835 --> 00:00:15.821
Files cross trust boundaries in web applications, APIs, scripts, data
pipelines, automation, and background workers.

3
00:00:15.821 --> 00:00:21.711
A file is not safe because it has a familiar name, a common extension, or
came from an internal workflow.

4
00:00:21.711 --> 00:00:34.725
Strong designs constrain file access to intended locations, generate safe
server-side names, validate content for its intended use, protect uploads,
manage permissions, separate storage areas, and avoid unsafe parsing.

5
00:00:34.725 --> 00:00:44.176
They also handle temporary files carefully, stream or limit large files,
avoid accidental overwrite, and protect what happens when files are served
back to users.

6
00:00:44.176 --> 00:00:48.834
Frameworks and libraries can help, but they do not replace ownership and
design.

7
00:00:48.834 --> 00:00:58.217
Developers still need to decide what files are allowed, where they live,
who can access them, how they are parsed, how long they remain, and what
evidence is logged.

8
00:00:58.217 --> 00:01:04.312
Security reviewers need to understand the workflow, the trust boundary,
and the downstream processing.

9
00:01:04.312 --> 00:01:05.956
The goal is not to fear files.

10
00:01:05.956 --> 00:01:12.600
The goal is to make file operations explicit, bounded, authorized,
validated, logged, tested, and maintainable.

11
00:01:12.600 --> 00:01:22.120
When file handling is designed as part of the application rather than
added as an afterthought, Python systems become safer, easier to operate,
and easier to review.
